On the absence of licences
--------------------------

None of the three carries an explicit licence. In the absence of a grant,
default copyright applies. The position taken here is deliberately conservative:

  * No third-party source, weights or data are copied into this repository or
    into any artifact it publishes. The competitor folders are inputs on disk,
    not dependencies.
  * Each project is named as its authors named it, and described factually.
    Naming a system in order to report a measurement of it is ordinary
    comparative reference, not redistribution.
  * Every claim made about a third-party system in bench/MODELS.md and in the
    generated reports is traceable to a file and a line in that project, so an
    author reading it can check it.
  * The harness's blind mode (`make bench BLIND=1`) renders the entire
    comparison as "System A" through "System D" and produces numerically
    identical results. If an author asks not to be named, that switch is the
    answer and nothing else has to change.

If you are an author of one of these projects and something here is wrong or
unwelcome, the fix is a correction, not a quiet edit: open an issue and the
reports will be regenerated.


Where these systems are run at a disadvantage, and where they are not
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Recorded in full in bench/reports/input_parity.md. In summary:

  * All three were retrained on our training split using their own training
    code and their own published hyperparameters, because their shipped weights
    were fitted on their own synthetic data and would have scored our accounts
    badly for reasons that have nothing to do with the quality of their methods.
  * ARGUS-PRISM's SIM-swap signal has no source in our dataset and is passed as
    zero throughout. That costs it one of its six signals. It is a capability it
    has and this benchmark cannot exercise, not a weakness.
  * MULE_HUNTER's serving layer applies hard score floors by transaction amount.
    Those floors would have raised its score on precisely the large-amount cases
    this benchmark is made of. They are a demo artifact rather than part of the
    model, so they are left out -- a choice made against our own interest.
  * MULE_HUNTER is a batch model run in streaming mode by rebuilding its graph
    at every tick, which is more work than the benchmark owed it.


